# mm.display

A portable display lifecycle and packed-region interface. Graphics policy,
fonts, image decoding, and controller commands live above or below this module,
not inside it.

Pixels are row-major, and geometry's bits_per_pixel says how they are packed.
Two packings are defined.

One-bit pixels are byte-aligned per row: the most significant bit is leftmost,
one is white, zero is black, and padding at the visible right edge has no
portable meaning.

Sixteen-bit pixels are RGB565, most significant byte first, two bytes each and
no row padding. Colour is the caller's to compose: Color names the three a
portable smoke test can ask of any panel, and a provider maps them onto whatever
its panel needs, while anything else is pixels the caller writes.

write changes controller memory; refresh makes it visible. A panel that is
already showing what was written answers Ok and sends nothing, because the
caller asked for something already true. That keeps one portable sequence
working across an ePaper panel and a TFT.

The platform-interface marker makes a selected board display provider part of
an executable only when its ordinary closure reaches mm.display. Host tests may
replace the linkable Unsupported fallback directly.
